===Proper noun===
{{en-prop|head=[[united|United]] [[states|States]]|def=1}}

# {{senseid|en|Q30}} {{lb|en|in the singular, formerly also plural}} {{ellipsis of|en|United States of America}}
#* '''1793''' December 3, {{w|George Washington}}, [[s:George_Washington's_Fifth_State_of_the_Union_Address|Fifth State of the Union Address]]:
#*: As soon as the war in Europe had embraced those powers with whom the '''United States''' have the most extensive relations there was reason to apprehend that our intercourse with them might be interrupted and our disposition for peace drawn into question by the suspicions too often entertained by belligerent nations.
#* {{quote-book
|en
|year=1964
|author=w:John F. Kennedy
|chapter=The Immigrant Contribution
|title=w:A Nation of Immigrants
|url=https://archive.org/details/nationofimmigran0000kenn/
|edition=Revised and Enlarged
|publisher=w:Harper & Row
|LCCN=64-7830
|OCLC=899031989
|page=64
|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/nationofimmigran0000kenn/page/64/
|text=As we have seen, people migrated to the '''United States''' for a variety of reasons. But nearly all shared two great hopes: the hope for personal freedom and the hope for economic opportunity.}}
#* {{quote-av
|en
|year=1965
|author=w:Harry S. Truman
|title=MP2002-401 Former President Truman Discusses "The Buck Stops Here"
|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20210202073514/https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MDrzHjL4Goo
|archivedate=02 February 2021
|url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MDrzHjL4Goo
|publisher=w:Harry S. Truman Presidential Library and Museum
|id=National Archives Identifier: [https://catalog.archives.gov/id/595162 595162]
|time=0:26
|text=I used to have a sign on my desk that said, "The Buck Stops Here." The buck stops at the president's desk when he's president of the '''United States''', and he either makes the decisions or he lets them go by default, and you can't afford to do that when you're president.}}
#* {{quote-journal
|en
|date=April 29, 2012
|author=Nathan Rabin
|title=TV: Review: THE SIMPSONS (CLASSIC): “Treehouse of Horror III” (season 4, episode 5; originally aired 10/29/1992)
|url=http://www.avclub.com/articles/treehouse-of-horror-iii,72723/
|page=
|passage=“King Homer” follows the story of King Kong closely, with Mr. Burns taking the freakishly over-sized King Homer from his native Africa, where he lives proud as a simian god, to the '''United States''', where he is an initially impressive but ultimately rather limited Broadway attraction.}}
# {{lb|en|in the plural}} The collection of individual [[state]]s of the [[United States of America]].
#: {{ux|en|The Judicial power of the '''United States''' shall not be construed to extend to any suit in law or equity, commenced or prosecuted against one of the '''United States''' by Citizens of another State, or by Citizens or Subjects of any Foreign State. ({{w|Eleventh Amendment to the United States Constitution}}, 1794)}}
#* '''1781''', January 10, {{w|Samuel Huntington (Connecticut politician)|Samuel Huntington}}, letter to {{w|John Adams}}:<sup>[https://books.google.com/books?id=jkNHAQAAIAAJ&pg=PA229&dq=%22these+United+States%22&hl=en]</sup>
#*: Congress have no expectations from the influence which the people of England may have on the British counsels, whatever may be the disposition of that nation or their magistrates towards these '''United States'''.
# {{lb|en|nation's name}} Federal nation consisting of several states, actual, historical or hypothetical.
#: {{ux|en|Republic of the '''United States''' of Brazil.}}
#: {{ux|en|The '''United States''' of Europe.}}
#: {{ux|en|The '''United States''' of the Ionian Islands.}}

====Usage notes====
During the first few decades after independence authors commonly treated United States as a plural noun, but in contemporary English it is always singular.

